The current solution regressed to pure multi-restart greedy (makespan 334). The best prior version (score 3412.97, makespan 292) added first-improvement insertion local search plus ILS. I'll restore that approach: greedy gives a good starting schedule, insertion hill-climbing fixes ordering mistakes on the true makespan, and ILS perturbation escapes local optima — all bounded by an 80s deadline so the three workloads fit within 360s.

Step-by-step reasoning:
1. Greedy construction samples candidates and appends the txn minimizing actual makespan — a strong but locally myopic start.
2. First-improvement insertion moves (remove txn i, try all positions j) accept the first gain, saving expensive `get_opt_seq_cost` evaluations versus best-improvement.
3. ILS perturbs the incumbent with 2–4 random relocations and re-climbs, escaping local optima until deadline — this is what drove makespan 292 before.

